{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And you shall weave the tunic of fine linen, and you shall make the mitre of fine linen, and a girdle, etc.</b> The Sages said (Arakhin 16a) that the tunic comes to atone for the shedding of blood, and what does this have to do with that? For if it is because the tribes, the brothers of Joseph, dipped Joseph's tunic in blood — nevertheless, what relevance does the shedding of blood that occurs in every generation have to the tunic? For without doubt this atonement applies in every generation, and not to the shedding of blood by the tribes alone. And the author of the Akeidah was very hard pressed on this matter, as you may see in his book. Moreover, there was no shedding of blood there at all, for the fact that they dipped the tunic in blood is not shedding of blood — only in a far-fetched way is there an aspect of shedding of blood, for they sold him into servitude, and our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Bava Batra 8b): 'Capture — they all participated in it.' However, I found a resolution to this in what Rabbeinu Bachya wrote on parashat Vayeshev, and this is his language: 'On account of the tunic things rolled onward to the enslavement of their bodies in Egypt, and likewise afterward there were ten martyrs, for indeed the body is the tunic of the soul' — thus far his language. You see the opinion of the Rav that the body is the tunic and garment of the soul, and if so there is a likeness and relationship between this tunic and the body of one who is murdered. And it was entirely of fine linen — שֵׁשׁ, that is, flax — for Cain was the head of all murderers, and the matter rolled onward through the fact that he brought seed of flax and his offering was not accepted, and the offering of his brother Abel was accepted; therefore he grew jealous of him and killed him. And therefore there were six (שֵׁשׁ) cities of refuge, for 'שֵׁשׁ' falls upon the meaning of 'six (שִׁשָּׁה).'" "And the mitre of fine linen atones for arrogance of spirit, because the mitre was like a crown signifying authority, as it is written (Ezekiel 21:31), 'Remove the mitre and exalt the crown.' And it is written (Isaiah 62:3), 'And a diadem of royalty in the palm of your Elohim.' And it was made of fine linen (שֵׁשׁ), for fine linen is fitting for one who rises to greatness, as is written of Joseph (Genesis 41:42), 'And he clothed him in garments of fine linen (שֵׁשׁ).' And the girdle atones for the pondering of the heart; therefore the girdle was thirty-two cubits long, and the contemplation of transgression is more severe than the transgression itself, for the act itself — after a person has already done it — he is sated with the toil of his soul and his soul loathes it, but the contemplation cleaves to him forever, just as a belt cleaves to a man's waist; therefore the girdle, which cleaved to him like a wrapping, comes for atonement. 'And make for them linen breeches' — to atone for the uncovering of nakedness, a matter that is practiced in modesty when one is secluded alone, for 'בַד' in its meaning also carries the sense of solitude. And the priestly garments atoned for these eight transgressions: for four of them are the most severe among all transgressions that depend on deed and speech, and they are: idolatry, the uncovering of nakedness, the shedding of blood, and evil speech — corresponding to them all. And four of them are comprehensive categories: for the corruption of justice causes the destruction of the world, and arrogance of spirit and contemplation of transgression and brazenness of forehead encompass all transgressions, and they are the most prevalent." "And perhaps this was the intent of the convert, who said: I will convert on condition that they make me High Priest, so that I may wear these garments — as is found in tractate Shabbat (31a). Now was he really such a fool that he wanted to convert for the sake of wearing these garments? Rather, surely he had heard the voice of scribes reading 'And these are the garments which they shall make' (Exodus 28:4), together with the explanation of their intent — what they atone for — and the convert knew about himself that he had transgressed all of these in his gentile state and he needed all of these atonements. And it appears that this was also the intent of Ahasuerus, who donned priestly garments, as they learned in tractate Megillah (12a) through a gezeirah shavah: it is said here 'the glory of his greatness' (Esther 1:4) and it is said below 'for honor and for glory' (Exodus 28:2) — just as below, priestly garments, so too here, priestly garments. For he too erred in this, thinking that wearing them would serve him as atonement for all eight of these categories of transgression. And we may further settle with this what our Sages of blessed memory expounded in tractate Megillah (12a) on the verse 'And the one near to him, Carshena, Shethar, Admatha' etc. (Esther 1:14). They said: The ministering angels said before the Holy One, blessed be He: Did they perhaps bring before You yearling bulls and two turtledoves upon an altar of earth? And Tarshish — did they perhaps serve before You in priestly garments that contain tarshish, shoham, and yashfeh? Meres marsena — did they perhaps press before You with blood and meal-offerings, etc.? And it appears to me to explain why, of all the priestly garments, he mentioned only the breastplate in which the stones were set, and of all of them he mentioned only tarshish — because in all the priestly garments the sanctity was not as refined as in the breastplate, in which were the Urim, the explicitly pronounced Name, and in which there were twelve stones; and he alluded to all of them with tarshish, which is trei shesh — twice six; twice six — behold, twelve." "Now this midrash lacks explanation, for one may ask: why did they not bring accusations, saying: Did they perhaps receive the Torah? Do they perhaps don tefillin and the like? Rather, surely because they were not commanded concerning these, therefore there is no grounds to blame them for this. But if so, they were likewise not commanded concerning sacrifices either, so how could the angels bring accusations against them for not having brought bulls, etc.? Moreover, one must examine the language closely: why did the angels speak in a negative formulation — 'Did they perhaps bring before You' — and why did they not say simply, 'Surely they do not bring offerings before You'? Rather, surely the essence of the accusation was that the angels saw Ahasuerus wrapping himself in a mantle that was not his and donning priestly garments, and furthermore that he drank from the vessels of the Sanctuary, as our Sages of blessed memory learned from the verse (Esther 1:7), 'And vessels of diverse kinds' — this wicked man was using the Temple vessels (Megillah 12a). Therefore the angels found an opening to confound his feast and to bring accusations against him, saying before the Holy One, blessed be He: 'Master of the Universe, see how this wicked man has donned priestly garments as if he were a priest offering sacrifices — but did he bring before You bulls and turtledoves? And see how he has donned tarshish, trei shesh, holy of holies it is to HaShem (Exodus 28:36) — but did he perform with them the service of HaShem, blessed be He?' And when they said (Esther 1:14) 'Meres marsena,' they picked a quarrel with him over the fact that he grew drunk from holy vessels: 'But did he press in them blood and meal-offerings in accordance with the law of these holy vessels? Rather, he used all of them for his own needs — and if so, he deserves a great punishment, for the stranger who offers shall be put to death' (Numbers 18:7). And with this the language 'Did they perhaps bring' is precisely accounted for, as is clear to every discerning person." "But we still have grounds to lodge a complaint against the author of this midrash, for how did he expound 'Carshena, Shethar, Admatha' as though all this were the words of the angels, when the language of the verse does not convey that — for it is stated: 'the seven chamberlains who saw the king's face' (Esther 1:10), implying that all these are names. For how can the beginning of the verse be joined to its end? Moreover, it is difficult: why was Ahasuerus in fact not punished as Belshazzar was punished for using the holy vessels? And furthermore, what possessed Ahasuerus to act thus, to use holy vessels — did he not see or know what happened to Belshazzar? And yet another difficulty: for our Sages of blessed memory said (Megillah 12a) on 'And the drinking according to the law' (Esther 1:8) — that means according to the law of the Torah: eating more abundant than drinking, like the eating and drinking of the altar — a bull and three tenths of an ephah of fine meal for the altar's eating, and half a hin of wine for drinking. And what possessed Ahasuerus to act thus? For is it not the way of the world that at every feast the drinking is more abundant than the eating — and 'go out and see what the people do'! And why would Ahasuerus deviate from the custom of the world to pattern his feast after the eating of the altar?" "And it seems to me to say regarding this that Ahasuerus sought to rectify two things through it. The first is this: we find in several places that there is a great feast whose table is full, while the table of his Master is empty — an opening is given to the prosecutors to say: 'Master of the Universe, see — from all these feasts, did he ever offer up to the On High a bull or a ram?' — as is concluded in the Yalkut, pericope of וַיֵּרָא (Genesis 22:1) ״אַחַר הַדְּבָרִים הָאֵלֶּה״, that the angels were prosecuting and saying: 'From all these feasts that Abraham made, did he ever offer before You a bull or a ram, etc.?' So too was Ahasuerus afraid lest they prosecute his feast in the heights: 'Did he offer from the entire feast to the On High bulls or doves?' Therefore he erred in his imagination, thinking that everything would be set right when he made his feast in the manner of the feasts of the altar, and it would be accounted to him as atonement as though he had literally offered upon the altar. And therefore he took the holy vessels, and clothed himself in priestly garments, and made eating more abundant than drinking, and gave his eunuchs names after the names of the offerings — for he thought that all this would rise for him as a pleasing offering, as though everything were done upon the altar in truth.\nThe second is the opposite of this: his main intent was to clothe himself in priestly garments for atonement, for it had been told to him by the sages of Israel that these eight garments atone for known transgressions. So he wished to adorn himself in them for honor and splendor, and in holy vessels too he wished to adorn himself, for nothing like them was to be found, as they learned from the verse ״וְכֵלִים מִכֵּלִים שׁוֹנִים״ (Yalkut Shim'oni Esther 1, §1058). And in any case it was told to this wicked man that it is forbidden for strangers to wear these garments, but only a priest at the time of service, and also regarding the holy vessels he feared the punishment that happened to Belshazzar. Therefore he thought to rectify this and said: Granted, Belshazzar erred and erred grievously, for he used these vessels not in their customary manner — for their manner of use is that eating be more abundant than drinking, whereas regarding Belshazzar it is written (Daniel 5:1) ״לָקֳבֵל אַלְפָא חַמְרָא שָׁתֵי״, and if so he surely multiplied drinking more than eating, and he did everything in order to distance the vessels from their sanctity, to use them not in their customary manner — therefore he was punished. And further he thought that he was punished because from the entire feast he did not offer a bull or a ram, etc." "But Ahasuerus thought to be clear of all these, for he said: I will use these vessels in their customary manner — eating more abundant than drinking — and then I shall not profane them from their sanctity. In this too I am clear, and my kingdom is clear, from the wearing of priestly garments, for it is as though I stand and offer offerings, and through this he would also be clear from the prosecution of 'Did he ever offer before You a bull or a ram?'\nAnd in order to strengthen his error, he gave names to his eunuchs who served him at the feast — those who sat first in his kingdom — after the names of the priests, the ministers of our Elohim in the Holy Temple, for they are first in the kingdom of Heaven, those who see the face of the Supreme King. Therefore he called those who saw his face, who were close to him, by names: Karshena, Shetar — after the names of bulls of the year and two doves, and so all of them. And all this he did to equate his feast and its attendants in every respect to the feasts of the altar and to the service of the priests, the ministers of the altar, and he thought to be exempt through this — both from the prosecution of 'Did he ever offer before You a bull or a ram?', and from the punishment of Belshazzar, and from the punishment of wearing the garments by a stranger. And because all this was imagination alone and there was no actual service there, therefore the angels found room to prosecute and say: Master of the Universe, do not turn to their offering — it is a vain offering, for there is nothing in all his deeds but imagination alone, for there was no actual offering there at all. Therefore they said in language of negation: ״Did they offer before You bulls of the year or doves, etc.?״ — and merely because his attendants were called by these names, should it be considered as though he literally offered?\nAnd in this manner the entire explanation of the verse ״וְהַקָּרוֹב אֵלָיו כַּרְשְׁנָא שֵׁתָר״ (Esther 1:14) will be exactly according to its plain sense, even according to the words of the author of the midrash. And in any case the author of the midrash said according to his own reasoning that the angels prosecuted — for in truth they had room to prosecute and to charge him with all the charges mentioned, and presumably the angels of mercy did not hold back from mentioning his obligation for the good of Israel and to turn his mischief back upon his own head — for clothing himself in priestly garments without the offering of a sacrifice, and for not offering a bull or a ram from the entire feast, and for using holy vessels. Therefore his feast was spoiled through Vashti; but in any case he was not killed like Belshazzar, for he was close to inadvertent sin, and erred in this device which he thought — that his entire feast was of the manner of above. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {6 "The verse citation at the start of comment [1] appears in the Hebrew as אסתר א:יד, matching the normalized format already used in the body of comment [0]; retained consistently. The Yalkut Shim'oni citation number (§1058) follows the numbering visible in comment [0]'s תתרמח."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:52.400882883Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}